//// Decision 21's T2 — a field read whose receiver carries no type inference
//// could place. A record is `{TypeAtom, F1, …, Fn}` on erlang, so the read is a
//// POSITION, and the position comes from the field's name alone when nothing
//// else names the record. The name only identifies a record when nothing else
//// declares it — and "nothing else" is every record the PROGRAM declares, not
//// the ones this file happens to import.
////
//// Here `main` imports `Ctx` (whose `rest` is field 1) and never imports `Hit`
//// (whose `rest` is field 0), and the value in hand is a `Hit` reached through
//// the optional binder, which has lost its type. Counting only what this file
//// imports makes `rest` look unique, and the read lands one slot over: erlang
//// printed `1` — the length of `entry`, a neighbouring field — where commonJS
//// printed `2`, with nothing said about it. When the two declarations sit one
//// slot further apart the same guess reads past the tuple and the program dies
//// with `{error, badarg}` instead of answering wrongly; both are the one defect.
////
//// wasm is not in `targets`: the wasm backend resolves a field to an offset the
//// same way, and closing that row is its own front's.

pub mod shapes;
import {Ctx, hitOf} from "shapes";

pub fn main() {
    var n = 0 - 1;
    if (hitOf()) { h -> n = h.rest.length; };
    @print(n);
    @print(Ctx(a: "1", rest: []).rest.length);
}
